    affiliation: "Department of Nuclear Engineering, North Carolina State University, Raleigh, NC 27695, USA"
abstract: >-
  Positron annihilation spectroscopy (PAS) has proved to be a highly sensitive and
  non-destructive analytical technique for characterizing open-volume defects and
  free-volume voids in materials for decades. Over the years, specialized
  routines have been developed to analyze annihilation radiation in both time and
  energy domains, yet most classical analysis codes were developed decades ago
  and became challenging to run and maintain on modern operating systems.
  Meanwhile, experimental workflows are often scattered among unrelated tools,
  without a shared interface or cross-tool data exchange. To address these
  limitations and lower the learning barrier, this paper presents Positron Lab
  Suite (PLS), a comprehensive PAS data analysis platform built with a graphical
  user interface and modern python packages. PLS comprises four major modules
  that provide intuitive workflows for positron annihilation lifetime
  spectroscopy (PALS), Doppler Broadening Spectroscopy (DBS), Coincidence DBS,
  and variable-energy depth-profiling, together with automation and diagnostic
  tools. Built-in simulators can generate synthetic PALS spectrum and
  depth-profile data for testing fitting methods and evaluating their
  reliability. In this paper, the architecture and functionalities of PLS are
  discussed with validation through case studies.
keywords:
  - positron annihilation spectroscopy (PAS)
  - positron annihilation lifetime spectroscopy (PALS)
  - Doppler broadening spectroscopy (DBS)
  - coincidence Doppler broadening spectroscopy (CDBS)
  - variable-energy positron depth profiling (VEP)
  - data analysis software
